      { col1: 'Best for',                  col2: 'Value, primate + wildlife combo', col3: 'Luxury, shorter treks, Rwanda-only trip' },
    ],
    tableHead: { col1: 'Factor', col2: 'Uganda', col3: 'Rwanda' },
  },
  {
    heading: 'Permit Cost',
    body: [
      'The most significant difference between the two countries is permit cost. Uganda\'s standard permit is USD 800 for foreign non-residents, dropping to USD 600 in April and May. Rwanda\'s permit is USD 1,500 — nearly double Uganda\'s standard price.',
      'For budget-conscious travellers, Uganda is the clear winner. A family of four saves USD 2,800 on permits alone by choosing Uganda over Rwanda. For a couple the saving is USD 1,400 — enough to cover several nights\' accommodation.',
      'Rwanda\'s USD 1,500 permit is justified by the country\'s premium positioning as a luxury destination, but the gorilla experience itself — one hour with a habituated family — is not meaningfully different at twice the price.',
    ],
    tip: 'If cost is a significant consideration, Uganda wins decisively. If you are already spending USD 500+ per night on a lodge, the USD 700 permit difference may be less relevant to your decision.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'Trek Difficulty and Duration',
    body: [
      'This is where Rwanda has a genuine practical advantage. Volcanoes National Park in Rwanda has a more compact terrain and the gorilla families are generally located at lower altitude and shorter distances from the trailhead than in Bwindi.',
      'Rwanda trekking times average 1 to 2 hours to find the gorillas. Uganda\'s Bwindi treks average 2 to 4 hours, with some lasting 6 to 8 hours on long days. Bwindi\'s forest is genuinely dense, steep and demanding.',
      'For older travellers, those with physical limitations, or anyone with limited time, Rwanda\'s shorter and generally easier treks are a meaningful advantage. That said, hiring a porter in Uganda significantly reduces the physical demands, and many seniors complete Bwindi treks comfortably.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Rwanda: shorter, less strenuous treks on average',
      'Uganda (Bwindi): more variable, can be short or very long',
      'Uganda: porters available and recommended',
