      'Both countries: gorilla sighting success rate above 99%',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Number of Gorilla Families',
    body: [
      'Uganda has significantly more habituated gorilla families available for tourism. Bwindi alone has 19 families across four trekking sectors (Buhoma, Ruhija, Nkuringo and Rushaga). Mgahinga has one family. Total Uganda capacity: 20 families, up to 160 visitors per day.',
      'Rwanda\'s Volcanoes National Park has 12 habituated families. Daily capacity is therefore lower, and availability can be tighter during peak season.',
      'For travellers flexible on trek date, Uganda\'s larger number of families means more permit availability and easier last-minute bookings. Rwanda\'s 12 families sell out faster.',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'What Else Can You Do?',
    body: [
      'This is perhaps the most compelling reason to choose Uganda for many travellers. Uganda offers the complete East African safari experience alongside gorilla trekking: chimpanzee tracking in Kibale Forest, big game drives in Queen Elizabeth and Murchison Falls, the Nile boat safari, Ssese Islands, Lake Bunyonyi and more.',
      'A classic 7-day Uganda itinerary covers gorilla trekking in Bwindi, chimpanzee trekking in Kibale, and game drives in Queen Elizabeth — three world-class wildlife experiences in a single trip.',
