const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can I do gorilla trekking in both Uganda and Rwanda on the same trip?', a: 'Yes. This is a popular combination. Bwindi in Uganda and Volcanoes National Park in Rwanda are separated by approximately 4 hours of driving via the Cyanika border crossing. A typical combined itinerary spends 2 nights in Bwindi, crosses to Rwanda, and spends 1 to 2 nights at Volcanoes. Allow 5 to 6 days minimum for this circuit.' },
  { q: 'Which country has better gorilla photography conditions?', a: 'This is subjective and depends on the specific gorilla family encountered. Rwanda\'s families are often found in bamboo forest and open glades which can provide better light. Bwindi\'s families are typically in denser forest with lower light levels. For serious gorilla photographers, the Gorilla Habituation Experience in Uganda (USD 1,500, 4 hours with the gorillas) offers the best conditions of any option.' },
  { q: 'Is Rwanda safer than Uganda for tourists?', a: 'Both countries are considered safe for tourists. Rwanda has an exceptionally low crime rate and is frequently cited as one of the safest countries in Africa. Uganda is also generally safe for tourists visiting national parks, with the same standard travel precautions that apply across East Africa. Neither country presents unusual safety concerns for safari visitors.' },
];

